# koblas working notes

Kotlin Multiplatform BLAS and LAPACK.

## Pre-PR gate

Run `./gradlew check lintDocs`.

- check runs tests and some light checks.
- lintDocs runs detekt and dokka linting.
- For new/edited files add `--rerun-tasks`, detekt caches per source-set and skips them otherwise.
- The default run pins `koblas.backend=reference`. Add `-Pkoblas.hostTests=true` for the OpenBLAS and
  SuiteSparse bindings, and `-Pkoblas.noSimd=true` on top for the scalar thresholds, which is the only
  way the level-2 bindings and the small-size dispatch paths are reached.

## Tests

- Test names within backticks, plain ascii, describing the behavior under test.
- Class almost always named after the file under test (`<FileUnderTest>Test`).
- One observable behavior per test; assert behavior, not implementation. Follow arrange → act → assert.
- Mirror existing tests' framework, layout, and assertion style.
- Reuse existing fixtures/helpers; don't add new ones unless asked.
- Use the parameterized form for variants of the same behavior; separate tests for semantically different ones.
- Keep comments minimal and let the test name carry the intent.
- No JVM test over 300ms.
- Backtick names take words only: `checkNativeSafeTestNames` rejects `(`, `)` and `#`, and a comma fails to
  compile on JS and native.
- Check a backend routine against the portable reference, not against hand values. The
  `assert*AgreesWithReference` helpers are the pattern, and a new backend joins them.
- `wellConditioned` is diagonally dominant, so it factors to an identity pivot. A test about pivoting or
  row permutation must perturb it or it exercises nothing.

## Bench

Read `koblas-bench/README.md` before benchmarking. In short:

- `./gradlew :koblas-bench:jvmLevel3Benchmark` runs one suite, prefixed by target: `jvm`, `linuxX64`
  or `macosArm64`. Only the host's own target runs, so macOS numbers come from a macOS checkout.
- Cite a crossover or a speedup in KDoc only when it came from these suites; drop the number otherwise.

## Design

- Public types carry their element type: `F64DenseMatrix`, `F64Blas`. A second element type brings its own
  registry and seams rather than widening these.
- Dense storage is column-major, entry `(i, j)` at `i + j * rows`. Sparse is CSC with rows strictly
  ascending within a column, which the constructors validate rather than repair.
- The portable reference is the semantic definition. A host binding gates on a measured threshold and falls
  back to it, so every routine agrees with it to within rounding.
- Backends register per half into a seam keeping the strongest offer, ties to whoever registered first.
  Register during startup, before other threads run.
- An `*Into` destination may alias its input, and that test is on the backing buffer, not the wrapper.

## Style

- Prefer clear naming and types over comments. Comments explain *why* (soundness, perf, lifecycle) and never
  restate the code. Never narrate changes, avoid "now/no longer/previously", and don't cite issues. Keep `TODO`/`FIXME`.
- KDoc on public API only. Use functional notation for math (`A(i, j)`, not `A[i][j]`); use `[Symbol]` for links.
- Don't edit anything under `.github/` unless asked directly.
- Single-line conventional commits, no scope, no body/trailers:
  `feat: `, `fix: `, `chore: `, `refactor: `, `docs: `, `test: `.
- PR body: follow `.github/pull_request_template.md`, with `Closes #NN` on its own line at the top.
